       "[{'query': 'What does Tamara Anderson do for a living?',\n",
       "  'correct_id': '35',\n",
       "  'query_id': 0,\n",
       "  'keys': [{'key': ' Meet Tamara Anderson, a special kind of teacher who helps kids like you communicate better! She\\'s worked with lots of little ones, from tiny tots taking their first steps into big kid territory all the way up to high schoolers working on more complex conversations and reading skills. You might wonder what makes her such an expert? Well, she spent many years studying and learning about speaking, listening, and reading so that she could help others do it even better themselves.\\n\\nNow, let\\'s break down some fancy words you might hear when people talk about what Tamara does. They call it \"speech-language pathology,\" but really, it\\'s just helping people find their voice and express themselves clearly. Imagine being unable to tell your friends exactly which game you want to play at recess – frustrating, right? That\\'s where someone like Tamara comes in handy!\\n\\nThere are two main parts to what Tamara does: evaluation and therapy. Evaluation means figuring out if there are any areas where a child needs extra support in communicating. It\\'s like taking a test, only instead of answering questions about history or math, kids show how they speak, listen, and read. This lets Tamara understand how best to assist them.\\n\\nTherapy is the fun part where Tamara gets to teach new ways of talking, listening, and reading! Just like sports coaches create drills and exercises to improve athletic performance, Tamara creates games and activities tailored to each child\\'s unique needs. These activities make practicing these essential skills enjoyable and rewarding. \\n\\nAs a bonus, Tamara also works as a coach for other speech-language pathologists (SLPs), the official name for professionals doing similar work. By sharing her wisdom and experience, she ensures even more kids can get the help they need to thrive. Plus, she consults with educators to promote better communication, language, and literacy strategies in classrooms.\\n\\nSo why does any of this matter to YOU, dear reader? Communication skills are vital tools for navigating life now and in the future. Being able to share your thoughts and feelings confidently will open doors to friendships, academic achievements, and career opportunities. And guess what? All those things lead to one extraordinary result: building successful lives!',\n",
       "    'key_id': 35},\n",
       "   {'key': \" Meet Kristin Bradley-Bull, a helper who works with non-profit organizations. Non-profit organizations are special kinds of businesses that don't make money for themselves; instead, they use any money they earn to help others or make the world a better place. Think of groups like animal shelters, food banks, or clubs that teach kids new skills - those are all non-profits!\\n\\nNow, what does Kristin do exactly? Well, she helps these non-profit organizations come up with strong plans and strategies so they can achieve their goals more effectively. It's kind of like when you have a big project at school, and you create an outline or a step-by-step plan to follow to complete it successfully. That's what Kristin does, but for non-profit organizations!\\n\\nShe also focuses on developing staff capacity, which means helping the people working at these organizations become even better at their jobs. By doing this, everyone involved can work together towards creating positive change in their communities and beyond.\\n\\nBut here's the cool part: Kristin doesn't just stop at non-profit organizations. She has a coaching practice where she supports individuals too! Imagine having someone guiding you to clarify your dreams and ambitions while keeping them aligned with your personal values. That's precisely what Kristin offers through her coaching. And guess what? This isn't limited only to adults; anyone looking for guidance and support to reach their potential can benefit from her expertise.\\n\\nLet me give you an example to show how amazing Kristin's work is. Suppose there's a small group of friends who want to start a community garden in their neighborhood. They might need assistance figuring out things like finding land, getting seeds, teaching others about gardening, and distributing fresh produce. With Kristin's help, they could learn how to create a solid strategy and build connections within their community. Over time, their little garden could grow into something much bigger, providing healthy food options and educating many neighbors about sustainable living.\\n\\nYou see, by empowering both organizations and individuals, Kristin contributes to transformative changes happening around us every day. Whether it's fighting against inequality, protecting our environment, or simply bringing joy to people's lives, her efforts inspire countless acts of kindness and improvement across various aspects of society. So next time you think about pursuing your passion or dream, remember that folks like Kristin are always ready to lend a hand and guide you along the way.\",\n",
       "    'key_id': 8558},\n",
       "   {'key': ' Once upon a time, there was a young Indigenous girl named Kaiya who lived in a beautiful community in Ontario, Canada. She loved going to school every day and learning new things. One day, her teacher told the class that it was time to start thinking about what they wanted to do when they grew up. The class had lots of questions!\\n\\nKaiya asked, \"What does \\'work\\' mean?\"\\n\\nHer teacher explained, \"Work means doing something you enjoy that helps others in your community. Some people become doctors, teachers, firefighters, artists, or engineers.\"\\n\\nThen, another student raised his hand and said, \"I heard that robots might take our jobs one day. Is that true?\"\\n\\nThe teacher nodded and said, \"That\\'s a good question. Technology like robots and computers will change some jobs, but they will also create new ones. And even if machines can do certain tasks, humans will always be needed for creativity, problem solving, and taking care of each other.\"\\n\\nNow, Kaiya was curious about college and university. \"Teacher,\" she asked, \"what happens after high school? Do I just go straight to work?\"\\n\\n\"No, not necessarily,\" replied the teacher. \"Many people choose to continue studying at colleges or universities. These places teach them more advanced skills so they can get better jobs. In fact, there are special programs called \\'co-op\\' and \\'internship\\' where students learn by working alongside professionals while earning credits towards graduation!\"\\n\\nKaiya thought this sounded exciting! But then, she wondered, \"How would I pay for all those classes and books?\"\\n\\nThe teacher smiled and answered, \"There are ways to make higher education affordable. Governments provide grants and scholarships based on academic achievement, leadership qualities, sports abilities, or cultural background. Additionally, most universities and colleges offer financial assistance packages tailored to individual needs. This help ensures everyone has access to quality education regardless of family income.\"\\n\\nFinally, Kaiya asked, \"Why is it important for me to think about my future career now?\"\\n\\nThe teacher looked around the room and saw eager faces waiting for an answer. He responded, \"Choosing your path early gives you plenty of time to explore different fields, build relevant skills, and develop connections with mentors who can guide you along the way. By starting now, you set yourself up for success in whatever job market awaits you – whether it includes robots or not!\"\\n\\nFrom that day forward, Kaiya couldn\\'t wait to grow up and discover which amazing profession she would pursue. With determination and support from her community, nothing could stop her from achieving greatness!',\n",
       "    'key_id': 8376},\n",
       "   {'key': ' A few years ago, a user named \"throwawayaccount123\" posted on a Reddit forum about an unusual experience they had while serving as the Poet Laureate of their small town. As a young queer poet who was passionate about social justice, throwawayaccount123 brought a fresh perspective to the role and used their platform to raise awareness about important issues like LGBTQ+ rights and climate change.\\n\\nHowever, things took an unexpected turn when the local government decided to cut funding for the arts program that supported the Poet Laureate position. This left throwawayaccount123 without any financial support for their writing and activism.\\n\\nDetermined to continue using their voice for good, throwawayaccount123 turned to their niche interest - knitting. They started selling handmade scarves and hats at farmer\\'s markets and craft fairs, donating a portion of the proceeds to local LGBTQ+ organizations. Their unique business model quickly gained attention from local media outlets, and before long, throwawayaccount1',\n",
       "    'key_id': 2192},\n",
       "   {'key': \"Meet Aisling, a Senior Client Manager at a public relations firm called Drury. But what does that mean? Well, let's break it down!\\n\\nPublic Relations (PR) is all about managing how a company or organization is perceived by the public. This can involve creating positive news stories, handling crises, and maintaining a good relationship with the media. Think of PR professionals like Aisling as the face and voice of a company. They work behind the scenes to shape its image and communicate its message to the world.\\n\\nNow, let's talk about Aisling's role as a Senior Client Manager. At Drury, she works closely with important clients such as Musgrave, Irish Life, Mars, Three, and The Local Enterprise Office. Her job is to understand their needs and goals, then develop and implement effective PR strategies to help them succeed.\\n\\nOne key aspect of Aisling's job is media relations. That means she builds strong connections with journalists who cover various fields, like business, technology, health, and entertainment. By understanding these reporters' interests and deadlines, she knows when and how to share her clients' stories to get maximum coverage. Imagine being able to call up your favorite reporter and pitch them a cool idea for a story – that's just part of Aisling's day!\\n\\nBefore working at Drury, Aisling gained valuable experience at other PR firms, where she honed her skills in strategy and reputation management. This involves carefully planning out campaigns to protect and enhance her clients' images. For example, if one of her clients faces a scandal or negative attention, Aisling would step in to manage the situation and minimize any potential damage to the company's reputation.\\n\\nIn short, Aisling is a pro at shaping how people see companies and organizations through creative storytelling and careful messaging. And while this might sound like a big responsibility, remember that every great achievement starts somewhere – even for future Senior Client Managers like Aisling!\",\n",
       "    'key_id': 1368},\n",
       "   {'key': \"Once upon a time, there was a woman named Brenda Stones. When she was younger, just like you, she loved to learn new things and explore the world around her. As she grew up, she discovered a special love for words and stories. She wanted to share this love with others and help them learn too!\\n\\nBrenda started working at a place called Cambridge University Press when she finished college. It was a big building filled with books and smart people who helped make those books. They taught her all about how to choose good stories and pictures, put them together, and share them with the world. This made her very happy because she knew that soon, lots of kids would be able to enjoy these stories and learn something new!\\n\\nAs Brenda continued learning more about making books, she moved to different places where they made even more kinds of books. Some were for little ones like you, some were for bigger kids, and some were for grown-ups. Each book had its own special purpose, teaching important lessons or sharing fascinating tales. Everywhere she went, Brenda met wonderful authors who wrote amazing stories and illustrators who created beautiful art.\\n\\nOne day, Brenda decided it was finally time to write her own stories! So, she began creating books for well-known publishers such as WHSmith, HarperCollins, Badger Publishing, and Franklin Watts. With each new project, she gained more experience and became better at crafting captivating narratives. Before long, she found herself helping other writers improve their work too!\\n\\nNowadays, Brenda still loves writing stories and helping others do the same. Sometimes, she teaches classes at universities so young people can learn what she knows about making great books. Other times, she shares her wisdom through workshops and seminars. In fact, if you ever become interested in writing your own stories one day, chances are you might meet someone like Brenda!\\n\\nOh, did I mention? Just like our dear friend Brenda, YOU have the power to create fantastic worlds and teach valuable lessons through your own stories! You see, we're never too old (or young) to start learning and growing. And isn't that simply marvelous?\\n\\nSo, here's a question for you: What kind of story would you like to tell someday? Think about it; maybe it will be the beginning of your very own adventure!\",\n",
       "    'key_id': 1210},\n",
       "   {'key': ' Meet Franklyn!\\n\\nFranklyn is a college student who loves learning about philosophy – that\\'s the study of how people think and understand things like truth, beauty, ethics, and reality. It might sound complicated, but it can help us answer some big questions we have about life! For example, why is it important to be honest? Or what makes something right or wrong? These are all questions philosophers try to answer.\\n\\nAt college, Franklyn even got a special certificate in dispute resolution. This means he learned how to help people solve arguments and problems together by talking and finding solutions that work for everyone involved. Isn\\'t that cool? Just imagine being able to help your friends when they have disagreements!\\n\\nBesides studying philosophy, Franklyn is also interested in accounting or law. Accounting is about managing money and making sure it\\'s used fairly and wisely. Law is about understanding rules and agreements so that everyone follows them and treats each other fairly. Both topics are essential because they keep our communities running smoothly.\\n\\nNow you might wonder, \"What does Franklyn do with all this knowledge?\" Well, he puts it into action by working at a place called Brownsville Think Tank Matters, Inc., or BTTM for short. BTTM is a special kind of non-profit organization, which means they use their resources to help others and improve the community without trying to make a profit. They focus on empowering young people like Franklyn to become leaders and create positive change in their neighborhoods.\\n\\nAs part of his job at BTTM, Franklyn supports violence interruption programs. Have you ever heard of peer mediation before? It\\'s similar to what Franklyn does, but instead of helping adults resolve conflicts, peer mediators help kids find peaceful ways to handle disagreements. By doing this, Franklyn helps reduce violent situations and creates a safer environment for everyone in his community.\\n\\nBefore joining BTTM, Franklyn took part in several volunteer and internship opportunities focused on social justice and community service. Social justice means treating everyone fairly and equally, no matter who they are, where they come from, or what they believe in. Community service involves giving time and energy to help others and make the world a better place.\\n\\nOne such opportunity was through the CUNY Service Corps, where Franklyn worked on projects that benefited his local community. Another one was as a member of the Northern Manhattan Improvement Corporation, which aimed to enhance the quality of life for those living in northern Manhattan. Through these experiences, Franklyn gained valuable skills while contributing to meaningful causes.\\n\\nAdditionally, Franklyn served as a Peer Success Coach as part of the Student Academic Success Program (SASP). As a coach, he helped fellow students achieve academic success by offering support and guidance. This experience further solidified his passion for assisting others in reaching their full potential.\\n\\nAfter finishing college, Franklyn plans to continue making a difference in his community. By combining his interests in philosophy, accounting, or law, and community service, Franklyn demonstrates that anyone can contribute positively to society, regardless of age or background. You too can start making a difference today—even small actions count!',\n",
       "    'key_id': 1425},\n",
       "   {'key': ' Meet Dr. Wendy K. Chung, a very smart doctor who studies genes! Genes are like tiny blueprints that tell our bodies how to grow and work properly. Just like you might use instructions to build something, your body uses genes to know what it needs to do.\\n\\nDr. Chung works with patients who have special medical conditions caused by changes in their genes. These kinds of conditions can affect many different parts of the body, such as the muscles, heart, or even how we process food. By studying these conditions closely, Dr. Chung helps doctors understand why they happen and find better ways to treat them.\\n\\nOne example of her work involves something called \"newborn screening.\" When babies are born, hospitals check them for certain health problems so they can get treatment right away if needed. Dr. Chung worked on developing a way to test babies for a condition called spinal muscular atrophy (SMA). SMA affects the muscles that help us move, talk, eat, and breathe. Now, because of her hard work, this important test is done all over the country!\\n\\nAnother part of Dr. Chung\\'s job is learning about how genes play a role in common diseases like obesity and diabetes. Obesity means having too much body fat, while diabetes makes it difficult for the body to control blood sugar levels. Both of these conditions can lead to other serious health issues down the line. To learn more, she compares the genes of healthy people to those with obesity or diabetes, hoping to uncover clues about what causes these diseases.\\n\\nBut wait – there\\'s more! Dr. Chung also looks into how genes impact the heart and lungs. For instance, she investigates congenital heart disease, which occurs when a baby is born with a problem in their heart\\'s structure or function. Additionally, she dives deep into understanding things like cardiomyopathies (heart muscle disorders), arrhythmias (irregular heartbeats), and long QT syndrome (a disorder affecting the electrical activity of the heart). And guess what? All of this information can ultimately contribute to finding cures or improving treatments for various illnesses!\\n\\nAs you can see, being a clinical and molecular geneticist is quite an adventure! It takes brains, creativity, and determination – just like solving any mystery. So next time you think about cool careers, remember Dr. Wendy K. Chung and the amazing world of genes!',\n",
       "    'key_id': 6624},\n",
       "   {'key': \" Chapter 10: Karen Hollman - An Environmental Activist through Occupational Health\\n\\nImagine working in a factory where your boss doesn't care about whether you get sick from toxic fumes or heavy machinery accidents. Unfortunately, this was the reality for many workers in the past. But today, there are people who fight for worker's health and safety, like our heroine, Karen Hollman!\\n\\nKaren Hollman is an occupational health expert who helps companies keep their employees healthy and safe at work. You might wonder what 'occupational health' means? It simply refers to the health risks and concerns related to one's job or profession. For example, construction workers may face injuries due to heavy equipment, while office workers could develop eye strain or carpal tunnel syndrome because of sitting at computers all day.\\n\\nNow let me tell you how Karen became an environmental activist through her line of work. Imagine if factories released harmful chemicals into the air or water near your home; it would affect everyone's health, right? That's why Karen focuses on both worker safety inside the company and protecting the environment outside it too. This way, she ensures that businesses take responsibility for their impact on both their employees and Mother Nature.\\n\\nOne significant aspect of Karen's job is conducting medical screenings for employees. These tests help identify any potential issues early so they can address them before causing severe harm. Think of these screenings like regular checkups at the doctor but specifically designed for each person's specific job duties.\\n\\nAnother essential part of Karen's role is creating programs called EAPs – Employee Assistance Programs. These support systems assist employees dealing with various challenges such as mental health issues, substance abuse problems, or even family troubles. By offering resources to handle these matters, companies create happier, healthier environments, boosting productivity overall!\\n\\nBut wait, there's more! Remember when I mentioned Karen wanted to protect the world beyond the workplace? To do this, she encourages organizations to follow strict guidelines known as 'medical surveillance.' Medical surveillance makes sure that employers track and prevent hazardous exposures by regularly monitoring their facilities and processes. So instead of waiting until someone gets hurt, proactive measures are taken to ensure no damage occurs in the first place!\\n\\nLastly, Karen specializes in helping integrate disabled individuals into the workforce seamlessly. Her efforts break down barriers, allowing everyone equal opportunities regardless of physical abilities. What a champion for inclusivity!\\n\\nIn conclusion, being an environmental activist isn't just about picketing or protesting. Sometimes, change comes from within industries themselves. Through her dedication to occupational health, Karen Hollman fosters safer workplaces and greener practices, proving herself a true friend to both employees and Earth itself. And guess what? YOU can become an advocate like Karen too! Always remember to think critically about the connection between jobs and environmental impacts, encouraging those around you to consider sustainable choices every day.\",\n",
       "    'key_id': 7193},\n",
       "   {'key': 'Once upon a time, there was a girl named Jackie who loved being outside and playing sports. She grew up in a town called Oakville, but one day she moved all the way across Canada to British Columbia. There, she found a job helping people make parks and playgrounds! Even though she wasn\\'t a big grown-up yet, she knew a lot about how to make spaces where kids like you can run around and have fun.\\n\\n\"What do you mean by \\'landscape architect?\\'\" you might ask. Well, imagine if you had a big box of building blocks, and someone asked you to build a special place just for running and jumping. You would think about what kind of ground to put down so it\\'s soft and safe, or maybe even bumpy and exciting! Then, you\\'d decide where to put tall towers, bridges, and slides - everything a kid needs to have a great time playing. That\\'s exactly what Jackie does, only instead of blocks, she uses plants, rocks, dirt, and water!\\n\\nYou know how your favorite park has sandboxes, swings, and climbing structures? Or maybe even a little stream where you can watch tadpoles wriggle around? All those things were planned out and built by people like Jackie! They spend lots of time figuring out the best places to plant trees, dig ponds, and lay paths. This makes sure everyone using the space will be happy and safe.\\n\\nWhen Jackie isn\\'t dreaming up awesome new parks, she likes to go on adventures exploring nature. Sometimes she goes mountain biking or hiking with her friends. One thing that makes Jackie really special is something called \"gravity sports.\" These are activities like skiing and snowboarding, where you slide down hills really fast! But because Jackie wants to share these experiences with everybody, she also loves learning about adaptive equipment that helps people with disabilities enjoy gravity sports too. In fact, before moving to British Columbia, she spent many years teaching kids with disabilities how to enjoy the mountains safely.\\n\\nNow you might wonder, \"How did Jackie learn how to help others while having fun?\" When Jackie was younger, she saw some of her own family members struggle with mobility challenges. Watching them find creative solutions inspired her to study both engineering and architecture. By combining these two fields, she learned how to create amazing spaces that anyone can access and enjoy! So remember, no matter what obstacles come your way, keep trying, stay curious, and soon enough, you\\'ll discover cool ways to overcome anything!',\n",
