       "   {'key': 'Once upon a time, in a small town named Harmonyville, lived two best friends, Timmy and Sally. They loved playing together, laughing, and sharing stories. However, one day, they noticed that their friend Billy seemed sad and didn\\'t want to play anymore. Timmy and Sally decided to talk to Billy about it.\\n\\n\"Billy, what\\'s wrong? Why don\\'t you want to play with us?\" asked Timmy.\\n\\nBilly looked down and said, \"I feel bad about myself. I think I\\'m not good enough.\"\\n\\nSally hugged Billy and said, \"That\\'s not true, Billy! You are special just the way you are.\"\\n\\nTimmy nodded and added, \"Yes, we all have things that make us unique and wonderful!\"\\n\\nJust then, Mrs. Johnson, the wise neighborhood doctor, walked by and saw the kids talking. She approached them and asked if everything was okay.\\n\\n\"We\\'re worried about Billy. He doesn\\'t seem happy,\" explained Sally.\\n\\nMrs. Johnson smiled and said, \"It sounds like Billy might be feeling a little blue. Sometimes, our feelings can get hurt, or we may feel uncomfortable in our own skin. It\\'s essential to remember that everyone has days when they feel this way, even grown-ups.\"\\n\\n\"What can we do to help Billy feel better?\" asked Timmy.\\n\\n\"Well,\" started Mrs. Johnson, \"we can remind him of all the fantastic things he can do and how much his friends love him. We can also encourage him to express his feelings through words, drawings, or any other fun activity he enjoys.\"\\n\\nShe continued, \"And sometimes, when someone feels really sad or scared for a long time, they might need help from a professional who knows lots of ways to make people feel happier and safer. Just like how your teacher helps you learn new things in school, these professionals help people understand and manage their emotions.\"\\n\\n\"Oh, like Ms. Thompson, our school counselor!\" exclaimed Sally.\\n\\n\"Exactly!\" replied Mrs. Johnson. \"Now, let\\'s go back to playing and show Billy how much fun we can have together.\"\\n\\nFrom that day forward, Timmy, Sally, and their friends made sure to include Billy in their games, always reminding him of his worth and value. And whenever anyone felt sad or unsure, they knew they had each other—and kind adults like Mrs. Johnson—to turn to for support and guidance. Together, they learned that every emotion mattered, and taking care of themselves and others created a beautiful place called Harmonyville.',\n",
